# Mirror and Water Images

> Hold a figure to a mirror and every point jumps across an invisible
> axis; flip it face-down in water and every point flips across a
> horizontal line. Two reflections, one idea: **each point moves to
> an equal distance on the opposite side of the axis**. Master the
> axes and letters, and these become the fastest marks in the
> non-verbal section.

## 1. The Two Reflections

[definition]
A **mirror image** reflects a figure across a **vertical axis**
(the mirror stands upright): left becomes right, right becomes left;
top and bottom stay. A **water image** reflects across a
**horizontal axis** (the water surface): top becomes bottom, bottom
becomes top; left and right stay.
[/definition]

| | Mirror (vertical axis) | Water (horizontal axis) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Left–right | swapped | unchanged |
| Top–bottom | unchanged | swapped |

[important]
The memory hook: a mirror flips **across**, water flips **along** —
but never both. If an option has both left-right AND top-bottom
inverted, it is a 180° rotation, not a reflection, and is the
classic wrong answer planted in every question.
[/important]

## 2. Mirror Images of Letters and Numbers

Letters that look the same in a vertical mirror (their mirror image
is unchanged): **A, H, I, M, O, T, U, V, W, X, Y**.

Letters that do NOT mirror to themselves (they turn into reversed
shapes): **B, C, D, E, F, G, J, K, L, N, P, Q, R, S, Z** — the
right-facing parts (B's bumps, L's foot, K's arms) swing to the
other side.

Digits unchanged in a vertical mirror: **0, 1, 8** (1 only in
simple fonts). Every other digit becomes a *reversed* version of
itself — a backwards 2, a backwards 3, and so on. Careful: mirrored
digits are NOT rotations (6 does not become 9 in a mirror); exam
options deliberately include the rotated twins as traps.

**Worked example.** The mirror image of **MOM** is **MOM** — M, O
and M are each self-mirroring, and the sequence is palindromic.
But **PAN** mirrors to a visually reversed **NAP**: each letter is
reflected AND the letter order reverses — two changes to track,
not one. Reflect letter by letter, then read the sequence
backwards.

## 3. Water Images of Letters and Numbers

In idealised block form, the letters with horizontal (top-bottom)
symmetry — **B, C, D, E, H, I, K, O, X** — keep their water images
unchanged. The clear changers are **A, F, L, P, T, U, V, Y**, whose
tops and bottoms swap; each still reads as a flipped version of
itself, never as a different letter.

Among digits, **0, 1, 8** survive the water flip unchanged, while
3, 5, 7 and 9 flip into near-unrecognisable shapes — the flipped 9
resembles a misshapen 6, which examiners exploit. When in doubt,
write the character on paper and fold along the horizontal axis.

## 4. Clocks and Time in Mirrors

[definition]
Mirror-clock shortcut: the mirror time reads **11:60 − H:MM** (or
equivalently 12:00 − time). Between 11 and 1 o'clock mirrors, use
**23:60 − time** when needed. Water-image time: **18:30 − H:MM**
(17:90 in some books for hour-precision) — i.e. subtract from
6:30/6:90.
[/definition]

**Worked example.** A clock shows 4:30. Its mirror image shows
11:60 − 4:30 = 7:30. Check: 4:30 + 7:30 = 12:00 — mirror times of a
12-hour dial always sum to 11:60 (i.e., 12 hours minus the
reflection offset).

**Worked example (water).** The water image of 4:30: subtract from
18:30 → 14:00 → on a 12-hour dial, 2:00. (Top of the clock becomes
the bottom: 12↔6, 3↔9 swap across the horizontal.)

## 5. Figures: Reading the Reflection

For line drawings, reflect piece by piece:

```text
Figure:               Mirror image:
   |\                    /|
   | \                   / |
   |__\                 /__|
   (arrow points          (arrow points
    bottom-right)          bottom-left)
```

Method:

1. Find the axis the question states (usually the mirror is drawn
   to the right of the figure — axis vertical).
2. Flip the most asymmetric feature first (an arrowhead, a dot, a
   tick-mark) — it eliminates two options instantly.
3. Check distances: gaps preserved, sizes preserved, angles
   preserved. Reflection never resizes.
4. Count elements in each region if the figure has internal lines —
   counts must match region by region.

## 6. Combined Operations

Some questions reflect twice: mirror then water. **Mirror + water
in succession equals a 180° rotation** — a fact worth knowing
because options often include the intermediate (mirror-only) state
as a trap.

Sequence questions show a figure and its reflection across changing
axes: alternating vertical/horizontal axes generate the pattern
mirror → rotate180 → mirror → rotate180...

## 7. Traps and Tactics

- **Rotation traps:** a 180° rotated option (both flips) when only
  the mirror is asked; a 90° rotated option for water questions.
- **Rebuild traps:** one small internal mark correctly reflected but
  the outline redrawn wrong (or vice versa) — verify outline and
  detail separately.
- **Letter-order traps:** in word images, the ORDER of letters also
  reverses (MOM stays MOM only because each letter mirrors and the
  sequence is palindromic); **PAN** mirrors to a visually reversed
  **NAP** — reflect each letter AND read the sequence backwards.
- **Time-saving order:** eliminate by (1) obvious wrong axis, (2)
  the most asymmetric detail, (3) internal counts.

## 8. Summary

Mirror = flip across a vertical line (swap left–right); water =
flip across a horizontal line (swap top–bottom); both together =
180° rotation. For letters, memorise the self-mirroring set
(A H I M O T U V W X Y) and treat everything else as reversed.
Clocks: mirror time = 11:60 − actual; water time = 18:30 − actual.
For figures, flip the most asymmetric detail first, then verify
counts — reflection preserves every distance, area and angle except
orientation.
